h throughput  library prep workflow  um which is depicted below which um you  you have your edited genomic dna and  then your pool of primers  and that red circle in those pools kind  of  indicates that these are blocked primers  and so you do two rounds of pcr  for this um amplicon sequencing and so  the first round is  locus specific pcr where the rna sh2 is  present so this will  um allow you to to target many many  regions at once and not have the risk of  the primer dimers  pcr two is then doing your your  universal amplification adding on those  adapters and then these are sequence  ready to go for illumina sequencing and  then we have um  we have taken our internally developed  analysis pipeline and we are working now  with a third party to make this  available through a cloud-hosted  environment  so as i mentioned our our ramp seek  we have a ramp seek design pool design  tool  that um will support many different  reference genomes  you come with a bed a bed file and then  you can input this in  and you'll get designs for your up to  5000 assays  we'll let you know how many of these can  be pooled together  in what size panels and then showing um  some sequencing data here by using this  technology we're able to get very high  quality sequencing data and  looking at metrics um here as far as  percent mapped reads on target rates and  coverage uniformity for different panel  sizes  when we're using 10 or 50 nanograms of  in this case corial genomic dna samples  the  the set of bars on the far left of each  graph is a is a 19 plex  um assay panel so we've got 19 different  assays in that pool doing  just one pcr reaction the second  plot or second set of bars is for a 282  plex  and then a 994 plex using our high  throughput  protocol or our regular protocol and the  high throughput allows you to only have  to do one spry cleanup  um but you can see here you know great  levels of  uniformity and on target rate  so this is really really great and  allows you to  um do your library prep  in high throughput and so just to kind  of highlight back to that figure that i  showed when where we were developing the  hifi protein  so the figure that went into that  publication  used the ramp seek technology so in this  case we are using this technology to  validate  our high fidelity cast nine and so  that panel on the left where we see many  many off-target  sites you know those were all nominated  through  through guideseek and then we came back  and quantified this with ramp seek  so in this case we're looking at um in  all of the different  conditions we tried we are looking at  essentially over 1500 different  individual pcr reactions that we're able  to greatly reduce  by running these panels of assays  together  and so this is just really great for  reducing reagents reducing time and  able to produce great validation data  for um  for this high fidelity protein and the  ram seek technology  now has been published in um in many  different  journals now and this highlights on some  of the different publications where  people have used this technology to  assess their off target editing  um rampsy can also be really really  useful  for genomic screens or or large  knockout studies that you're doing um  because the ramp seek  design tool can do all of the pcr design  for you  you then do your delivery your library  prep  and you can do your data analysis and in  this case you know we used  that for when we were validating the  cast 12a ultra protein so we're looking  at  120 different sites and droquette cells  and 96 different sites and 293 cells  and trying to see at these different pam  sites how  much better ultra has gotten and we um  have done this all through our  ramp seek ngs analysis design library  prep  and our and our internal analysis tool  and so  um to mention that that  analysis tool um and kind of bring us  back to the workflow so again the first  piece is to to identify your off target  sites  and then to do 